  <div id="lessonContent">
    <h2>1. How Interviews Really Work</h2>
    <p>Most beginners fear interviews. Truth: Interview is NOT an exam. It is a conversation. Company wants to know 3 things:</p>
    <p>1. <b>Can you do the work?</b> - Show projects, write small code<br>2. <b>Will you learn fast?</b> - Show you learn by doing<br>3. <b>Will you stay?</b> - Show you are serious, not just trying</p>
    <div class="note"><b>Golden Rule:</b> Never say "I don't know" and stop. Say "I don't know this exact thing, but I know something similar..." - Then explain what you know. Interviewers hire attitude + basics, not perfect memory.</div>
    <h2>2. LIVE DEMO - Mock Interview - Practice Now</h2>
    <p>Click "Show Answer" after you try to answer in your mind. Practice out loud:</p>
    <div class="demo-card">
      <div class="mock-wrap">
        <h3 style="text-align:center;margin:0">🎤 Mock Interview - <span id="mockScore">Answered: 0 / 5</span></h3>
        <div class="mock-q"><b>Q1. Tell me about yourself.</b><button class="btn btn-next" style="padding:6px 12px;font-size:13px;margin-left:8px" onclick="showMock(1)">Show Answer →</button><div class="mock-a" id="mock1">"I am Ali, a Web Developer from Lahore. I have completed HTML, CSS and JavaScript course. I built 10+ projects - Portfolio, Shop Website with Cart, Weather App, To-Do App. I am looking for a Junior Frontend Developer job where I can learn and build real websites."</div></div>
        <div class="mock-q"><b>Q2. What is the difference between let, var and const?</b><button class="btn btn-next" style="padding:6px 12px;font-size:13px;margin-left:8px" onclick="showMock(2)">Show Answer →</button><div class="mock-a" id="mock2">var = Old, function scope, can redeclare. let = New, block scope, can change value. const = New, block scope, CANNOT change value. I use let for values that change, const for values that stay same. I never use var in new code.</div></div>
        <div class="mock-q"><b>Q3. What is Flexbox vs Grid?</b><button class="btn btn-next" style="padding:6px 12px;font-size:13px;margin-left:8px" onclick="showMock(3)">Show Answer →</button><div class="mock-a" id="mock3">Flexbox = One direction - Row OR Column - For navbar, buttons, small layouts. Grid = Two directions - Rows AND Columns - For full page layout, cards, galleries. I use Flexbox for navbar, Grid for product cards.</div></div>
        <div class="mock-q"><b>Q4. How do you make a website responsive?</b><button class="btn btn-next" style="padding:6px 12px;font-size:13px;margin-left:8px" onclick="showMock(4)">Show Answer →</button><div class="mock-a" id="mock4">With media queries - @media (max-width: 700px) - Change layout on small screens. Use flexible widths - % not fixed px. Test on mobile at 360px. Example: Desktop 3 columns → Mobile 1 column.</div></div>
        <div class="mock-q"><b>Q5. Write code to reverse a string in JS.</b><button class="btn btn-next" style="padding:6px 12px;font-size:13px;margin-left:8px" onclick="showMock(5)">Show Answer →</button><div class="mock-a" id="mock5">let str = "Ali";<br>let reversed = str.split("").reverse().join("");<br>// split = "Ali" → ["A","l","i"] | reverse → ["i","l","A"] | join → "ilA"<br>console.log(reversed); // "ilA"</div></div>
        <p id="mockMsg" style="text-align:center;font-weight:bold;color:#16a34a"></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <h2>3. Top 10 HTML Questions - Click to Reveal Answer</h2>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(1)">1. What is HTML? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa1">HTML = HyperText Markup Language. It is the structure of a website - Headings, paragraphs, links, images, forms. It is NOT a programming language - It has no logic, only structure.</div></div>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(2)">2. What are semantic tags? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa2">Tags that say what they MEAN - &lt;header&gt;, &lt;nav&gt;, &lt;main&gt;, &lt;section&gt;, &lt;footer&gt;. Better than &lt;div&gt; for SEO and screen readers. Google understands semantic tags.</div></div>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(3)">3. Difference between div and span? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa3">div = Block - Takes full width, starts on new line. span = Inline - Takes only needed width, stays on same line. div for sections, span for small text inside a line.</div></div>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(4)">4. What is the difference between id and class? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa4">id = Unique - One element only - #myId in CSS - Used in JS getElementById. class = Reusable - Many elements - .myClass in CSS - Used for styling many items same way.</div></div>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(5)">5. What is DOCTYPE? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa5">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt; tells browser - This is HTML5 document. It must be the FIRST line. Without it, browser goes to old quirks mode and layout breaks.</div></div>
    <h2>4. Top 10 CSS Questions</h2>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(6)">6. What is Box Model? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa6">Every element is a box - Content (text) + Padding (inside space) + Border + Margin (outside space). Total width = Content + Padding + Border + Margin. box-sizing: border-box makes it easy.</div></div>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(7)">7. Difference between margin and padding? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa7">Margin = Space OUTSIDE the box - Between two elements. Padding = Space INSIDE the box - Between border and text. Margin pushes others away, padding makes box bigger inside.</div></div>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(8)">8. What is specificity? Which CSS wins? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa8">Order: Inline style (1000) > id (100) > class (10) > tag (1). Example: #title beats .title beats h1. !important beats all, but avoid it - It makes code messy.</div></div>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(9)">9. Difference between position: relative, absolute, fixed, sticky? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa9">relative = Moves from its normal place. absolute = Moves from nearest positioned parent. fixed = Stays on screen, does not scroll. sticky = Scrolls, then sticks at top - Best for navbar.</div></div>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(10)">10. What is z-index? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa10">Which element is ON TOP when they overlap. Higher z-index = On top. Navbar z-index: 100, so it stays above content. Works only on positioned elements (relative, absolute, fixed, sticky).</div></div>
    <h2>5. Top 10 JavaScript Questions</h2>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(11)">11. What is == vs === ? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa11">== checks VALUE only - "5" == 5 is TRUE (converts type). === checks VALUE + TYPE - "5" === 5 is FALSE. Always use === - Safer, no surprise conversion.</div></div>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(12)">12. What is hoisting? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa12">JS moves declarations to top before running. var x = 5; - Declaration is hoisted, value is NOT. That is why var can be used before line, but gives undefined. let and const are NOT hoisted the same way - They give error if used before.</div></div>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(13)">13. What is closure? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa13">A function that remembers variables from its outer function even after outer function finished. Example: Counter function remembers count. Used for private variables, timers, cart.</div></div>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(14)">14. What is DOM? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa14">DOM = Document Object Model - Browser turns HTML into a tree of objects. JS can read and change it - document.getElementById, innerText, innerHTML, style. That is how JS makes pages interactive.</div></div>
    <div class="qa-item"><button class="qa-q" onclick="toggleQa(15)">15. What is localStorage? +</button><div class="qa-a" id="qa15">Browser storage - Saves data even after page refresh or browser close. localStorage.setItem("name", "Ali") to save, getItem("name") to read. Limit 5MB. Used for cart, theme, To-Do list.</div></div>
    <h2>6. HR Questions - What to Say</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>HR Question</th><th>Good Answer</th><th>Bad Answer</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Tell me about yourself</td><td>Name + Skill + Projects + Goal - 1 minute, not life story</td><td>"I am... umm... I like..." - No structure</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Why should we hire you?</td><td>"I have built 10+ projects, I learn fast, I will deliver on time"</td><td>"Because I need a job"</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What is your weakness?</td><td>"I am new, but I learn by building. I built 10 projects to fix it."</td><td>"I have no weakness" / "I am lazy"</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What salary do you expect?</td><td>"For Junior Frontend, Rs. 40,000-60,000 is fair for my skills"</td><td>"Anything" / "Rs. 200,000"</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Where do you see yourself in 5 years?</td><td>"Senior Developer, leading projects, mentoring juniors"</td><td>"I don't know" / "In your chair"</td></tr>
    </table>
    <h2>7. Practical Task - What Companies Ask You to Build</h2>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Task 1 - Most common - 10 minutes</span><br>
<span class="text">// Make a button. On click, show count. Count increases each click.</span><br>
<span class="text">let count = 0;</span><br>
<span class="text">function increase() { count++; document.getElementById("num").innerText = count; }</span><br><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Task 2 - Filter list by search</span><br>
<span class="text">let names = ["Ali", "Ahmed", "Fatima", "Bilal"];</span><br>
<span class="text">let result = names.filter(n => n.toLowerCase().includes("a"));</span><br>
<span class="text">// filter() returns new array with only matching items</span><br><br>
<span class="comment" style="color:#94a3b8">// Task 3 - FizzBuzz - Classic interview question</span><br>
<span class="text">for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 15; i++) {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">if (i % 3 === 0 && i % 5 === 0) console.log("FizzBuzz");</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">else if (i % 3 === 0) console.log("Fizz");</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">else if (i % 5 === 0) console.log("Buzz");</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">else console.log(i);</span><br>
<span class="text">}</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>8. Homework - Prepare for Interview in 3 Days</h2>
    <p>Day 1: Learn all 30 questions in this lesson - Say answers out loud, not just reading<br>Day 2: Practice 5 practical tasks - Counter, To-Do, Filter, Calculator, Form validation - Without looking<br>Day 3: Mock interview with friend - Friend asks, you answer. Record yourself. Fix weak answers.<br>Then apply to 10 jobs - Rozee.pk, LinkedIn, Indeed - Junior Frontend / Web Developer.</p>
    <div class="box"><b>Remember:</b> Interview = Conversation, not exam | Never say only "I don't know" - Say "I don't know this, but I know..." | Tell about yourself = Name + Skill + Projects + Goal in 1 min | == value only, === value + type | Flexbox = 1D, Grid = 2D | Box Model = Content + Padding + Border + Margin | Specificity = Inline > id > class > tag | Practical tasks = Counter, Filter, FizzBuzz - Practice without looking</div>
    <p><b>Next Step:</b> <a href="/lesson50">Lesson 50 - Final Exam & Certificate</a> - Your final test. Pass it, get your certificate. You are almost a Web Developer!</p>
